data: {"item_id":"rs_149910","output_index":0,"sequence_number":157,"summary_index":0,"text":"The implementation seems mostly done. Need to ensure health endpoint works (already). Data persistence uses SQLite file path.\n\nPotential issues: Using import { open } from 'node:sqlite'; earlier we imported both sqlite and open. Node's sqlite built-in may not have named export open? In Node 22, I think the API is: import sqlite from 'node:sqlite'; then const db = await sqlite.open({ filename, driver: sqlite.Database }); So open is not a separate named export. In server.js we imported both sqlite and { open }. That's okay but we used open. In earlier patch we attempted to modify that, but currently code uses open from node:sqlite which may not exist causing error. Should adjust to use sqlite.open.\n\nLet's test server quickly.","type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"item":{"encrypted_content":"The implementation seems mostly done. Need to ensure health endpoint works (already). Data persistence uses SQLite file path.\n\nPotential issues: Using import { open } from 'node:sqlite'; earlier we imported both sqlite and open. Node's sqlite built-in may not have named export open? In Node 22, I think the API is: import sqlite from 'node:sqlite'; then const db = await sqlite.open({ filename, driver: sqlite.Database }); So open is not a separate named export. In server.js we imported both sqlite and { open }. That's okay but we used open. In earlier patch we attempted to modify that, but currently code uses open from node:sqlite which may not exist causing error. Should adjust to use sqlite.open.\n\nLet's test server quickly.","id":"rs_149910","summary":[{"text":"The implementation seems mostly done. Need to ensure health endpoint works (already). Data persistence uses SQLite file path.\n\nPotential issues: Using import { open } from 'node:sqlite'; earlier we imported both sqlite and open. Node's sqlite built-in may not have named export open? In Node 22, I think the API is: import sqlite from 'node:sqlite'; then const db = await sqlite.open({ filename, driver: sqlite.Database }); So open is not a separate named export. In server.js we imported both sqlite and { open }. That's okay but we used open. In earlier patch we attempted to modify that, but currently code uses open from node:sqlite which may not exist causing error. Should adjust to use sqlite.open.\n\nLet's test server quickly.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},"output_index":0,"sequence_number":158,"type":"response.output_item.done"}

